512:
The provisions of this article shall not apply to:
(1) Noncommercial filming or videotaping activities conducted solely for private or family use;
(2) Film activity conducted for use in a criminal investigation or civil or criminal court proceeding;
(3) Film activity conducted for news purposes;
(4) Film activity conducted for education, government, and public access and local origination for public television or public cable or public internet channels within the State of Georgia;
(5) Nonprofit 501(c) tax-exempt organizations and other governmental agencies if their film activity is not for commercial use; or
(6) Any film activity deemed by the Lumpkin County Board of Commissioners to be in the public interest.
Nothing in this article shall limit the right of the Lumpkin County Planning Department, sheriff, fire marshal, county clerk, or Lumpkin County Board of Commissioners to suspend film activity when deemed necessary for the protection of the public health, safety, or general welfare, or when such film activity poses an immediate hazard to persons and/or property.
(Res. No. 2026-18, Exh. A, 4-21-2026)

514:
(a) Before granting a film permit, the county clerk shall review the application and consult with the sheriff, fire marshal, Lumpkin County Planning Department, and the camera-ready liaison as appropriate, and determine that the proposed film activity will not unreasonably interfere with the public health, safety, and general welfare, or unreasonably endanger any property. Appropriate county officials shall be consulted to review the application as deemed necessary by the county clerk. In granting a film permit, any reasonable conditions may be imposed as necessary to protect public health, safety, general welfare, and property. Factors which may be taken into consideration in granting and conditioning a permit include, but are not limited to: noise, traffic congestion, traffic hazards, environmental problems, interference with street maintenance work, unreasonable interference with normal business operations or residential peace and quiet and whether the proposed activity would violate federal, state, or local law.
(b) Conditions which may be attached to the film permit may include, but are not limited to, the following:
(1) Requirements regarding the presence of governmental employees, such as law enforcement officers or firemen, at the permittee's expense;
(2) Requirements regarding traffic control, such as the placement of traffic control devices and signs at the permittee's expense;
(3) Requirements regarding cleanup and restoration of property used in the film activity;
(4) Restrictions regarding the use of firearms, or noise-creating or hazardous devices;
(5) Restrictions regarding stunts involving pyrotechnics, vehicle crashes, and other hazardous materials and/or activity;
(6) Requirements relating to partial or full nudity in public places;
(7) Restrictions regarding the time of the film activity; and
(8) Requirements regarding notifications to nearby businesses and/or residences.
(Res. No. 2026-18, Exh. A, 4-21-2026)
